Summary of Changes:
- Root-caused the abort via the CI workflow's own long-standing comment (.github/workflows/ci.yml, "Provide an audio device" step): every harness constructs SoundManager, which built a pool of 64 real sf::Sound objects at construction time; on a GitHub runner with no sound card this drove SFML's miniaudio backend to fail opening a playback device and SIGABRT before the harness's first check — which is why CI installs snd-dummy / a null ALSA device as a workaround instead of the game handling a missing device itself.
- Added SoundManager::m_audioAvailable (SoundManager.hpp), probed exactly once in the constructor via sf::PlaybackDevice::getDefaultDevice().has_value() (confirmed by reading SFML 3.0.2's vendored source that this call only enumerates devices and logs — never throws or aborts — even when no audio subsystem is present at all, so the probe itself is safe on a fully audio-less machine).
- When unavailable: skip constructing the sf::Sound pool (buffer decoding — dummyBuffer, m_fallbackBuffer — needs no device and still runs; only the pool of live sf::Sound objects does), and playSound()/playMusic() early-return as silent no-ops. Left stopMusic()/pauseMusic()/resumeMusic() unguarded deliberately: they operate on m_music, which stays in its never-opened default state when playMusic() never ran, and SFML already no-ops stop()/pause() on such an object — a guard there would be exactly the "scattered null-checks" R3 said not to add.
- Left the CI workaround in .github/workflows/ci.yml in place rather than removing it: the fix's own reasoning strongly implies it is now unnecessary, but that can only be confirmed by an actual device-less CI run (a shared, hard-to-revert consequence if wrong), which this session could not exercise. Added a comment pointing at the fix and stating the condition for safely removing it later.
