- No cast was kept in either file, and none was replaced by a switch on type or an enum type tag. The new virtual hooks carry the dispatch: Item::onPlayerTouch (+ ItemTouch), Block::onCharacterTouch (+ BlockTouch), Enemy::onPlayerTouch, Enemy::isHazardToEnemies, Entity::isOnGround, Entity::isPhysicsDriven, Entity::reversesAtLevelEdge, Entity::onTileImpact, Entity::ridesConveyors, Entity::applyHorizontalControl + beginPhysicsFrame + clearMovementRequests, Character::getCurrentMaxSpeed + suppressesGroundFriction, Player::getRunSpeed. Every one of them is an Open/Closed win: a new enemy, item, block or player character overrides a method instead of adding a branch to the physics core.
- Two sites deliberately use `getCategory() == EntityCategory::Player` with a guarded static_cast rather than a hook: coin-tile collection and the ceiling head-butt, both of which need a Player& to credit coins and score. That is not a fallback — the category is the EXACT answer where a dynamic_cast was merely sufficient (Player overrides getCategory() once for its whole subtree, Shadow Mario included, which is what preserves the pre-existing behaviour of both rules), it is one virtual call instead of an RTTI hierarchy walk, and it is the mechanism EntityCategory's own comment in Entity.hpp asks callers to use. resolveEntityVsEntity's existing category dispatch is untouched.
